= Replication — Simple =

'''Replication''' means doing a new study to see whether a scientific finding appears again.

The new study uses new observations or new data.

This is different from [[Reproducibility/Simple|reproducibility]].

Reproducibility often asks:

<blockquote>Can I use the same data and analysis and get the same result?</blockquote>

Replication asks:

<blockquote>If we investigate the scientific question again with new data, do we get a consistent finding?</blockquote>

Replication matters because one study can be affected by chance, measurement problems, a strange sample, or hidden bias.

A failed replication is important evidence, but it does not always prove the original result was false. The studies may differ in population, method, conditions, or statistical power.

Replication helps scientists discover whether a result is strong, fragile, or limited to certain conditions.

